<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/15th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Otlukbeli</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1473">1473</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Battle of Başkent</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Otlukbeli was fought on 11 August 1473 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a> in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> between the Ottoman sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehmed-ii">Mehmed II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a>. Ottoman artillery and firearms decided the day, ending Uzun Hasan's challenge to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> in Anatolia and his hopes of an effective alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-otlukbeli.jpg" alt="Battle of Otlukbeli" width="320" height="383" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Otlukbeli_savasi.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> opened negotiations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> in the autumn of 1463, and from 1464 he intervened in the affairs of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>. Mehmed II occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a> in 1468 but could not subdue the Turkoman tribes of the mountains reaching to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> coast, who rose from time to time around pretenders to the Karamanid throne. Uzun Hasan then turned more aggressive. He allied with the Venetians, made contact with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/knights-hospitaller">Knights of Rhodes</a>, the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a> and the Bey of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alanya">Alaiye</a>, and in 1472, with Karamanid forces, drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> from Karaman and marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bursa">Bursa</a> before Mehmed repulsed him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a> demanded Cappadocia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trabzon">Trabzon</a>, Mehmed answered with open defiance and, against his habit of secrecy, announced when he would march. The sultan reached Yenişehir on 11 April 1473; joined by Prince Mustafa, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karaman">Karaman</a>, and Prince Bayezid, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amasya">Amasya</a>, the army reached 85,000 men. Beyond <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sivas">Sivas</a> it crossed mountains in a snowstorm. Turahanoğlu Ömer Bey defeated an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> force of 5,000 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erzincan">Erzincan</a>, but on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a> the young Has Murad Pasha was drawn forward by a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feigned-retreat">feigned retreat</a> and his force was destroyed, a blow that badly demoralised the army.</p><p>On Wednesday 11 August the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> were caught in a narrow pass when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> troops appeared on the Otlukbeli ridge. Davud Pasha held off the commander Gavur İshak and engaged the right wing under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzun-hasan">Uzun Hasan</a>'s son Zeynel Mirza; Prince Mustafa then fell on Zeynel, who was killed. Another son, Uğurlu Mehmed, withdrew before Prince Bayezid almost without fighting. When Mehmed brought his 25,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/janissaries">janissaries</a> into action, Uzun Hasan fled, leaving a lookalike, Pir Mehmed Bey, in his place, and the battle turned into a rout.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Mehmed went on to take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sebinkarahisar">Şebinkarahisar</a>, whose lord surrendered, and announced his victory in a series of letters, among them a decree in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a> language to the Turkomans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, dated 30 August 1473. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> had nearly broken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu</a> power in the east; the dynasty was later destroyed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismail-i">Ismail I</a>, founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>, which became a new rival of the Ottoman Empire in the east.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Otlukbeli” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1473 Mehmed II marched east; after an initial Aq Qoyunlu success on the Euphrates, the Ottoman army, with its janissaries and field artillery, defeated Uzun Hasan decisively at the <b>Battle of Otlukbeli</b> on 11 August 1473.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aq-qoyunlu">Aq Qoyunlu · Military</a></li></ul></section>
